>> title: Ratatouille
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: vegetables, eggplant, zucchini, tomatoes, one-pot, provencal
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: ratatouille niçoise, ratatouille nicoise, Provençal vegetable stew, summer vegetable stew
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr 15 min
>> step.1: Each vegetable needs its own turn, not its own pan: eggplant first and alone, the tomatoes last.
>> step.2: fry 8 min, until it gives
>> step.3: fry 6 min more
>> step.4: soften 5 min
>> step.5: simmer uncovered 30 min
>> step.6: stand 15 min off the heat
>> step.7: Watery at the end means the lid was on or the tomatoes went in early. Uncovered over the heat brings it back.

The argument about ratatouille is whether each vegetable gets its own pan.

Fry @eggplant{1%large}(450 g; cut in 1-in cubes) in @olive oil{1/3%cup}(80 mL) in a #Dutch oven{} for ~fry{8%min}.

Fry @&(~1)eggplant{} with @zucchini{2%medium}(cut in half-moons), @red bell pepper{1%large}(cut in 1-in pieces) for ~fry{6%min}.

Soften @&(~1)vegetables{} with @yellow onion{1%large}(sliced), @garlic{4%cloves}(sliced), @thyme{4%sprigs}, @bay leaf{1} for ~saute{5%min}.

Simmer @&(~1)base{} uncovered with @ripe tomatoes{2%lb}(900 g; chopped, or canned whole tomatoes crushed by hand), @kosher salt{2%tsp} for ~simmer{30%min}.

Stand @&(~1)ratatouille{} off the heat with @basil{1/2%cup}(torn), @red wine vinegar{2%tsp}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp} for ~stand{15%min}.

Warm rather than hot is how this is eaten, and better the next day than the day it was made.
